---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---
<SiteLayout title="How this site is built — Vo Thanh Dat" path="/how-this-site-is-built/">
<a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<Header />
<main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="build-notes">
  <section class="section" id="top"><div class="container">
    <a class="text-link" href="/">← Back to portfolio</a>
    <p class="section-index build-eyebrow">Design & engineering</p><h1>How this site is built</h1>
    <p class="build-intro">A hands-on learning project in front-end development, UI/UX and accessible interaction, built with the same attention to delivery that I bring to infrastructure work.</p>
    <div class="build-facts"><span>Astro + TypeScript</span><span>Static HTML</span><span>GitHub Pages</span></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section" id="principles"><div class="container">
    <header class="section-head"><div><p class="section-index">01</p><h2 class="section-title">A clear reading path</h2><p class="section-desc">Make the profile easy to scan, then let readers choose the detail.</p></div></header>
    <div class="build-grid">
      <article><h3>Content before decoration</h3><p>Experience comes first. Technology is grouped by purpose, with optional links back to the roles where I used it. Supporting tools stay visible in two compact groups, with less emphasis than the core stack.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Layouts for the screen</h3><p>CSS grid adapts the experience and stack sections to the available space. Desktop uses section navigation; mobile uses a native dialog menu with large touch targets.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section" id="tokens"><div class="container">
    <header class="section-head"><div><p class="section-index">02</p><h2 class="section-title">One visual system</h2><p class="section-desc">Shared components and CSS tokens keep the pages consistent.</p></div></header>
    <div class="build-grid">
      <article><div class="build-palette" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div><h3>Colour with a purpose</h3><p>The four-colour mark and spectrum connect the personal identity with cloud, automation, reliability and security. Neutral surfaces keep the text readable in both themes.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Small, reusable pieces</h3><p>Astro components render the header, experience, stack, education and footer. Experience content uses a TypeScript interface, while CSS tokens define spacing, typography, colour and motion.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section" id="pipeline"><div class="container">
    <header class="section-head"><div><p class="section-index">03</p><h2 class="section-title">From source to static pages</h2><p class="section-desc">Build once and serve the generated output.</p></div></header>
    <ol class="build-pipeline"><li><span class="mono">01</span><h3>Source</h3><p>Astro components, typed content and assets.</p></li><li><span class="mono">02</span><h3>Build</h3><p>GitHub Actions installs locked dependencies and runs the Astro build.</p></li><li><span class="mono">03</span><h3>Publish</h3><p>The generated HTML, CSS and JavaScript go to GitHub Pages.</p></li></ol>
    <p class="build-caption">Static rendering fits this content-focused site. JavaScript handles theme selection, navigation, disclosures and copy feedback.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section" id="quality"><div class="container">
    <header class="section-head"><div><p class="section-index">04</p><h2 class="section-title">Interaction & verification</h2></div></header>
    <div class="build-grid">
      <article><h3>Keyboard and motion</h3><p>Semantic headings, a skip link, visible focus and native dialog/disclosure elements support keyboard navigation. Theme preferences are remembered, and reduced-motion preferences disable animated transitions.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Check the rendered result</h3><p>Review includes desktop and mobile layouts in both themes, image loading, horizontal overflow, menu focus, theme persistence and experience disclosures. Canonical metadata, a sitemap and a custom 404 support the published pages.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div></section>
</main>
<Footer /></SiteLayout>
